sses small AIRs using descriptor-array batched kernels\n   - All the batched CUDA FFI and orchestration logic\n\n3. In the caller (`prove_zerocheck_and_logup_gpu`), add routing logic that calls the batched handler first, then passes a skip mask to the existing function, and finally merges the results.\n\n4. Re-introduce CUDA kernels and FFI from reverted commit `0c699f47`.\n\nThis approach minimally modifies the existing function, isolates new batched logic in a separate module, and keeps the sensitive multi-stream Phase 2 code untouched. But I need to think through a subtlety: the existing Phase 1 does substantial precomputation like lambda powers and eq evaluations...\n\nActually, looking back at the research report, Phase 1 just builds work items by iterating through AIRs and sorting them by trace height for load balancing. The precomputed data like lambda_pows and eq_3b_per_trace comes from earlier in `prove_zerocheck_and_logup_gpu`, so those are already available to both paths.\n\nThe cleanest approach is to build all work items upfront, partition them into large and small groups, then route large items through the existing multi-stream path and small items through a new batched path. This avoids modifying Phase 1 entirely—I just need to extract Phase 2 into a reusable function.\n\nBut that still means refactoring the existing code... Let me think about a completely different strategy that doesn't touch the current implementation.\n\nI could precompute results for small AIRs using the batched kernel before calling the existing function, then run the existing function on all AIRs unchanged, and finally swap in the precomputed results afterward. Yes, small AIRs get processed twice—once efficiently via batching and once through the slow per-AIR path—but the existing function stays completely untouched, which eliminates optimizer risk. The redundant work on small AIRs is minimal since they're tiny anyway.\n\nBut wait, that's still wasteful. The batched path saves m